<worksheet>
    <title> Session #6 (Sections 2.6 and 3.1)</title>
    <p>
      Directions: Answer each of the following as best you can. Feel free to discuss your thoughts about the questions with some peers around you. 
      For additional practice problems and explanations, refer to the Math 142 textbook which is free and can be downloaded as a PDF 
      at <url href="https://oaktrust.library.tamu.edu/items/03980291-5e85-4d2e-a742-961ac49666aa"> Texas A&amp;M The OAKTrust Digital Repository </url>.
      <!--'&' and '<' are exceptional characters and are instead authored as &lt; and &amp;. See 4.1.4.2-->
    </p>
    
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Getting Warmed Up and in the Right Mindset</title>
    
    <exercise><!-- #1 -->
      <introduction>
        <p>
           Use the graph below to answer parts (a) through (d), writing <em>increasing</em> or <em>decreasing</em> in the blank.  
        </p>
        <image source="MATH142_HOGU_Session6_Fig1.png" width="70%">
          <shortdescription> Graph of a function with five highlighted points. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              Starting from the left, the graph is increasing, passing through a point labeled <m>A</m> and reaches a point <m>B</m>.
              The graph then decreases, passes through point <m>C</m> and reaches point <m>D</m>. 
              The graph then increases, passes through point <m>E</m>, and continues increasing out of the frame on the top.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </introduction>
      <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
               Between points D and E, the graph is <fillin characters="15" />.
            </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
               Between points A and B, the graph is <fillin characters="15" />.
            </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
               Between points C and D, the graph is <fillin characters="15" />.
            </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
               Between points B and C, the graph is <fillin characters="15" />.
            </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
    
    </paragraphs>
    
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Let Us Get Our Hands On</title>
  
    <exercise workspace="2in" number="1">
      <introduction>
        <p>
           A cubed shaped block of ice is melting such that its volume decreases at a rate of <m>2</m> cubic centimeters per minute. 
           How fast is the length of one side of the cube decreasing when the side length is <m>5</m> centimeters? 
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
               Step 1 (Draw a Picture): Sketch a picture that represents the problem and label any variables of interest.
            </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
               Step 2 (State Given &amp; To Be Determined Information): Identify (a) the given (or known) values for any variable or rate of change, 
               (b) the rates of change that need to be determined and at the specific values at which the rates need to be determined. 
               Be sure to include all units.
            </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
               Step 3 (Formulate Equations): Look at the information collected in steps 1 and 2 to find a formula that represents the relationships
               between the rates and the variables. <em>Hint: For this problem find the formula that relates the volume of a cube to the length of its side.</em>
            </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
               Step 4 (Take Derivative): Use implicit differentiation to take a derivative of the equation in step 3 with respect to time, <m>t</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
               Step 5 (Substitute): Substitute all known values (see step 2) into the equation found in step 4, and then solve for the unknown rate of change.
            </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      
    </exercise> 

    <exercise workspace="2in" number="2">
      <statement>
        <p> 
         The length of a rectangle is increasing at a rate of <m>4</m> meters per second, and the width is increasing at a rate of <m>2</m> meters per second.
         How fast is the area of the rectangle increasing when the length is <m>10</m> meters and the width is <m>6</m> meters? 
         <em>Hint: Follow the same steps as the previous problem! </em> 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise> 
  
    <exercise workspace="2in" number="3"><!-- #5 -->
      <statement>
        <p>
         A company's profit, in dollars, is given by the equation <m>\D P(x)=100x-0.5x^{2}</m>, where <m>x</m> is the number of units sold.
         If the sales are increasing at a rate of <m>10</m> units per day, how fast does the profit change when <m>50</m> units are sold?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise> 
  
  
    <exercise number="6"><!-- #6 -->
      <introduction>
        <p>
          The following are steps to finding increasing and decreasing intervals.
          <ol marker="1:">
            <li>
              <p>
                Determine the domain of <m>f</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find all partition numbers of <m>f'(x)</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Determine which partition numbers are critical values of <m>f(x)</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Plot all partition numbers on a sign chart.
                Put a solid dot on all critical values and an open circle on all partition numbers that are not critical values.
                Select one <m>x</m>-value in each interval, and evaluate <m>\D f\ '(x)</m> at each selected <m>x</m>-value to determine 
                whether <m>\D f\ '(x)</m> is positive or negative on each interval. Determine the behavior of <m>f(x)</m> on each interval using the sign of <m>f'(x)</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
          Given <m>\D f(x)=3x^{4}-8x^{3}+6x^{2}</m>, follow the four-step process outlined above to find the intervals on which <m>f(x)</m> is increasing and decreasing.
          After you are done, plug <m>f(x)</m> into a graphing calculator to verify your answer.
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Step 1:
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="2in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Step 2&amp;3: The partition numbers of <m>f'(x)</m> are <fillin characters="15" /> and the critical values of <m>f(x)</m> are <fillin characters="15" />.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="2in">
        <statement>
          <p>
           Step 4: <m>f(x)</m> is increasing on <fillin characters="15" /> and <m>f(x)</m> is decreasing on <fillin characters="15" />.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise> 

    <exercise number="7"><!-- #7 -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          <theorem xml:id="thm-local-extrema">
            <statement>
              <p>
                Suppose <m>f(x)</m> is continuous on an interval containing the critical value <m>x = c</m> and <m>f(x)</m> is differentiable near, 
                and on both sides of, <m>x = c</m>. Then, <m>f (c)</m> satisfies one of the following:
                <ul>
                  <li>
                    <p>
                      If <m>f '(x)</m> changes sign from positive to negative at <m>x = c</m>, then <m>f(c)</m> is a local maximum of <m>f(x)</m>.
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <p>
                     If <m>f '(x)</m> changes sign from negative to positive at <m>x = c</m>, then <m>f (c)</m> is a local minimum of <m>f(x)</m>.
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <p>
                      If <m>f'(x)</m> does not change sign at <m>x = c</m>, then <m>f (c)</m> is neither a local maximum nor a local minimum of <m>f(x)</m>.
                    </p>
                  </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            </statement>
            </theorem>
                <p>
                The first derivative sign chart for <m>\D f(x)=3x^{4}-8x^{3}+6x^{2}</m> (#6 above) is given below. 
                Use this chart and the above theorem to find the <m>x-</m>values of all local extrema for <m>f(x)</m>.
                                  
                <image width="100%">
                  <shortdescription>this is a short description</shortdescription>
                  <description><p>A table of the RNA codons.</p></description>
                  <latex-image label="scaling-tikz">
                  \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.75pt,y=0.75pt,yscale=-1,xscale=1]
                  %uncomment if require: \path (0,300); %set diagram left start at 0, and has height of 300

                  %Straight Lines [id:da15062745544499068] 
                  \draw [line width=1.5]    (152,91) -- (470,90) ;
                  %Straight Lines [id:da24334597009083647] 
                  \draw  [dash pattern={on 0.84pt off 2.51pt}]  (10,90) -- (152,91) ;
                  %Straight Lines [id:da9941703709593537] 
                  \draw    (152,60) -- (152,120) ;
                  %Straight Lines [id:da35116156909703444] 
                  \draw    (241,81) -- (241,100) ;
                  %Straight Lines [id:da5992193201402582] 
                  \draw    (354,80) -- (354,100) ;
                  %Shape: Circle [id:dp5915494726364277] 
                  \draw  [fill={rgb, 255:red, 0; green, 0; blue, 0 }  ,fill opacity=1 ] (236,91) .. controls (236,88.24) and (238.24,86) .. (241,86) .. controls (243.76,86) and (246,88.24) .. (246,91) .. controls (246,93.76) and (243.76,96) .. (241,96) .. controls (238.24,96) and (236,93.76) .. (236,91) -- cycle ;
                  %Shape: Circle [id:dp10698857422927244] 
                  \draw  [fill={rgb, 255:red, 0; green, 0; blue, 0 }  ,fill opacity=1 ] (349,90) .. controls (349,87.24) and (351.24,85) .. (354,85) .. controls (356.76,85) and (359,87.24) .. (359,90) .. controls (359,92.76) and (356.76,95) .. (354,95) .. controls (351.24,95) and (349,92.76) .. (349,90) -- cycle ;

                  % Text Node
                  \draw (50,48) node [anchor=north west][inner sep=0.75pt]   [align=left] {\textit{sign of} $f'( x)$};
                  % Text Node
                  \draw (33,95) node [anchor=north west][inner sep=0.75pt]   [align=left] {\textit{behavior of $f(x)$}};
